          <div class="rocket-shopify-article lngvty-article" style="max-width:880px;margin:0 auto;color:#181818;font-family:'Avenir Next',Avenir,'Century Gothic','Trebuchet MS',sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.75;letter-spacing:.15px;">

    <p style="max-width:760px;margin:0 0 24px;color:rgba(24,24,24,.68);font-size:20px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:.2px;">
        Facial pores do not have muscles to open or close, but managing oil flow, clearing trapped debris, and protecting skin from sun damage can make them look smaller.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:0 0 38px;padding:20px 22px;border-left:4px solid #181818;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            <strong style="font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.5px;">Quick answer:</strong>
            Pores do not have muscles, so no product, steam session, or cold rinse can physically open or close them. Pores look larger when excess sebum mixes with dead skin cells to stretch the opening, or when sun damage and ageing degrade the collagen that holds the pore walls upright. Clearing trapped oil with gentle chemical exfoliants, regulating sebum with niacinamide, and applying daily sunscreen to protect skin elasticity will tighten the appearance of pores, though individual pore size remains genetically fixed.
        </p>
    </div>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        If you look into a magnifying mirror under bright bathroom lighting, every pore on your nose and cheeks looks exaggerated. Social media filters and beauty marketing frequently promote the illusion of airbrushed, pore-free skin. In reality, pores are natural openings for hair follicles and oil glands that keep your skin barrier healthy. Nobody has poreless skin, and scrubbing aggressively to shrink them only creates irritation that makes the openings stand out more.
    </p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Pore visibility changes with weather, oil output, and hydration. In warm, humid Indian climates, sebaceous glands produce extra oil, creating surface shine that catches the light and emphasizes every follicle rim. Understanding why pores stretch allows you to ignore gimmicks that promise to close them and focus on daily steps that genuinely keep skin smooth and refined.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        The myth of opening and closing pores
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        The common belief that facial pores open with steam and shut tight under cold water is a persistent skincare myth. Human pores do not have muscular tissue around their rims.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26918966/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">1</a></sup> Without muscles to contract or expand, a pore cannot physically change its aperture on command.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Warm water and steam feel pleasant and soften hardened sebum, making daily cleansing more thorough. However, warmth alters oil viscosity rather than pore diameter. Splashing cold water causes a brief contraction of microscopic blood vessels near the skin surface. This creates a temporary sensation of tightness, but the physical size of the pore remains identical once skin returns to room temperature. Pulling on pores with adhesive strips or suction tools irritates the surrounding skin and does not change the size of the pore.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Why your pores look larger than they actually are
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        If pores cannot open and close, why do they appear noticeably larger at certain times? Dermatological research identifies three main biological causes that expand the follicle canal and create the optical illusion of huge pores.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26918966/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">1</a></sup>
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        The primary trigger is excess sebum flow. Clinical trials show a direct correlation between high sebum excretion and enlarged facial pores.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17034515/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">2</a></sup> When overactive oil glands pump out excess sebum, the oil combines with dead skin cells to form a dense plug that presses the follicle wall outward. Once exposed to oxygen at the surface, the plug oxidises into a dark blackhead, casting a sharp contrast that makes the pore look much wider than it is.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        The second driver is loss of structural collagen. Each pore is supported by a surrounding sheath of collagen and elastin fibers that hold the funnel upright. As we age, and particularly through cumulative ultraviolet sun exposure, those supporting fibers weaken. Without firm structural backing, round pores slacken and droop into elongated, oval indentations across the cheeks.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Surface dehydration worsens this effect. When skin lacks water, surface cells dry out and curl along their edges, creating a rough texture. While plump, hydrated skin reflects light smoothly, dehydrated skin creates shadows that pool inside each follicle depression, making pores stand out sharply.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:42px 0;padding:30px 26px;background:#181818;color:#FFFFFF;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#B87330;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">
