    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        A four-step daily routine to refine visible pores
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        A practical, barrier-friendly routine produces clearer, smoother skin without prompting compensatory oil production.
    </p>

    <ol style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:24px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 12px;">
<strong>Gentle cleansing.</strong> Wash with a mild cleanser and lukewarm water morning and night to remove excess oil and grime without inflaming follicle rims.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 12px;">
<strong>Apply active serum.</strong> Smooth 3 to 4 drops of Dark Spots, Pore Control Serum across damp skin .</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 12px;">
<strong>Lock in barrier moisture.</strong> Apply a lightweight, non-comedogenic moisturiser with ceramides to plump epidermal tissue and soften shadows.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 12px;">
<strong>Daily sunscreen defence.</strong> Apply broad-spectrum sunscreen of SPF 30 or higher every morning to prevent ultraviolet rays from degrading pore collagen.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Habits that make pores look worse
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        When pores seem prominent, quick-fix trends can be tempting. However, several common habits damage skin elasticity and make pores look more obvious over time.
    </p>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 12px;">
<strong>Adhesive pore strips.</strong> Peeling sticky strips tears healthy epidermal cells and stretches follicle rims, causing pores to loosen and refill quickly.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 12px;">
<strong>Extreme water temperatures.</strong> Steaming over hot water or rubbing ice cubes causes thermal stress and prompts rebound oil production.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 12px;">
<strong>Squeezing blackheads.</strong> Pinching clogs with fingernails ruptures follicle walls, causing inflammation, dark marks, and pitted scarring.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 12px;">
<strong>Over-exfoliating with acids.</strong> Layering multiple peeling solutions strips the moisture barrier, leaving skin dehydrated and making pores look rougher.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 12px;">
<strong>Magnifying mirrors.</strong> Examining skin inches away under harsh lighting creates false expectations. Real skin has texture and pores.</li>
    </ul>

    <div style="margin:34px 0;padding:24px 26px;border-top:1px solid #B87330;border-bottom:1px solid #B87330;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 7px;color:#B87330;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Keep in mind</p>
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            Pore size is determined by genetics and cannot be completely erased. The goal of a consistent routine is not to achieve impossible, airbrushed skin, but to keep pores clear of oxidised sebum and supported by firm collagen so they appear refined, smooth, and inconspicuous.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Realistic timelines for pore refinement
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Pore refinement takes time because skin renews gradually, and it needs weeks of steady care before you can see a difference. Products promising overnight pore erasure typically rely on temporary optical blurring primers or alcohol-based astringents that deliver no structural change.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Within one to two weeks, restored hydration plumps the surface and softens shadows. By four weeks, daily sebum regulation and gentle exfoliation clear surface pooling and oxidised blackheads, making pores look noticeably smaller and cleaner.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:56px 0 18px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Frequently asked questions
    </h2>
    <div style="border-top:2px solid #181818;">
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can you permanently shrink or close pores?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">No, pores cannot permanently shrink or close because they lack muscles and baseline size is genetic. You can visibly refine them by clearing trapped sebum and supporting collagen elasticity.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Does splashing cold water close pores after washing?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Cold water does not close pores. It temporarily constricts surface blood vessels, creating a brief feeling of tightness, but pore openings return to normal once skin warms up.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">How long does it take for enlarged pores to look smaller?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Initial improvements appear in two to four weeks as surface congestion clears. Results vary, and changes build with steady, daily use.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Why do pores on the nose look much larger and darker than on the cheeks?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">The nose has the highest density of active oil glands on the face. Excess sebum mixes with shed cells and oxidises at the surface into dark blackheads that create sharp contrast.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Will adhesive pore strips damage facial pores permanently?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Frequent use can damage the skin barrier and stretch the follicle opening. Repeated mechanical pulling weakens rim elasticity, leaving pores looser over time.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can dry skin have enlarged pores?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Yes, dry skin can show enlarged pores. When skin loses moisture and elasticity, the tissue around each follicle slackens, causing circular pores to sag into visible oval depressions.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can I use pore-refining serums if I am pregnant or breastfeeding?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Always check with your doctor before starting a new serum while pregnant or breastfeeding.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        References
    </h2>
